{
  "chalk": 1,
  "name": "05-strategies.chalk",
  "cells": [
    {
      "src": "Evaluation strategies",
      "type": "section",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "> [!goal]\n> Compare the classic answers to \"which redex first?\": what each finds, where each stops, and what each costs.",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "An interpreter has to pick a redex by a fixed rule. Two rules come to mind, and every programming language picks one: evaluate the argument before the call, as $f(g(x))$ does in most languages; or hand the argument over as it is, and evaluate it only where it is used.",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "> [!try]\n> $K = \\lambda x.\\, \\lambda y.\\, x$ throws its second argument away. Under each rule, what happens to $K\\ I\\ \\Omega$?",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "A term where the choice decides",
      "type": "section",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "Hand arguments over as they are, and $\\Omega$ is never touched:",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "normal: K I (omega omega)",
      "showWork": true,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "cbn: K I (omega omega)",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "Evaluate arguments first, and you never get past it. A step count shows how it goes round:",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "cbv 4: K I (omega omega)",
      "showWork": true,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "applicative: K I (omega omega)",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "Normal order is the safe choice, always:",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "> [!theorem] Standardization\n> If a term has a normal form, normal order reaches it.",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "The four classic strategies are the two rules, each with and without reducing inside a function body:",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "> [!definition] Strategies\n> - **Normal order** (`normal:`): the leftmost-outermost redex, also under λ, to the normal form.\n> - **Call by name** (`cbn:`): the leftmost-outermost redex, never under a λ, the argument passed unevaluated. It stops at a **weak head normal form**: a λ, or a variable applied to arguments.\n> - **Call by value** (`cbv:`): the function, then the argument, are reduced to **values** (a λ or a variable) before the call; never under a λ.\n> - **Applicative order** (`applicative:`): the leftmost-innermost redex, under λ too: call by value that goes all the way.",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "Where they stop",
      "type": "section",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "Call by name and call by value do not look inside a λ: a function is already a result, and its body runs when it is called.",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "cbn: λx. (λy. y) x",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "cbv: (λx. x) (λy. (λz. z) y)",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "What they cost",
      "type": "section",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "> [!try]\n> In $(\\lambda x.\\, x\\ x)\\ ((\\lambda y.\\, y)\\ z)$ the argument is used twice. Call by name copies it unevaluated; call by value evaluates it first. Predict where each stops.",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "cbn: (λx. x x) ((λy. y) z)",
      "showWork": true,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "cbv: (λx. x x) ((λy. y) z)",
      "showWork": true,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "Call by value did the argument's step once, first. Call by name copied the unevaluated argument, and stopped at $z\\ ((\\lambda y.\\, y)\\ z)$, a weak head normal form; normal order goes on into the copies and does the same step twice (lesson 4). Most languages call by value; Haskell calls by **need**: call by name that remembers an argument once it is evaluated, so it is done at most once, and never if unused.",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "In Lean",
      "type": "section",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "/-- Call by value treats variables and λs as values. -/\ndef isValue : Term → Bool\n  | app _ _ => false\n  | _ => true\n\n/-- One call-by-value step: the function, then the argument, become values before the call; never under a λ. -/\ndef cbvStep : Term → Option Term\n  | app (lam x b) a =>\n    if isValue a then some (subst x a b)\n    else (cbvStep a).map (app (lam x b) ·)\n  | app f a =>\n    match cbvStep f with\n    | some f' => some (app f' a)\n    | none => (cbvStep a).map (app f ·)\n  | _ => none\n\n/-- Take up to fuel steps with any strategy. -/\ndef run (step : Term → Option Term) : Nat → Term → Term\n  | 0, t => t\n  | n + 1, t => match step t with\n    | none => t\n    | some t' => run step n t'\n\ndef KIΩ : Term := app (app K I) (app ω ω)\n#eval (run betaStep 10 KIΩ).pretty   -- normal order: I\n#eval (run cbvStep 10 KIΩ).pretty    -- call by value: still evaluating Ω",
      "type": "lean",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "theorem cbvStep_lam (x : String) (b : Term) : cbvStep (lam x b) = none := by",
      "type": "exercise",
      "lean": true,
      "prompt": "Call by value does not reduce under a λ: a function takes no step.",
      "leanSolution": "  simp [cbvStep]",
      "hints": [
        "Unfold `cbvStep`: which clause does `lam x b` match?"
      ],
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "Exercises",
      "type": "section",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "Write the term the strategy stops at; it is compared up to bound names, not reduced further.",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "cbv: (λx. λy. y) ((λz. z) w)",
      "type": "exercise",
      "prompt": "Reduce $(\\lambda x.\\, \\lambda y.\\, y)\\ ((\\lambda z.\\, z)\\ w)$ by value.",
      "hints": [
        "The argument is reduced first, to $w$; then the call."
      ],
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "cbn: (λx. λy. x) ((λz. z) w)",
      "type": "exercise",
      "prompt": "Reduce $(\\lambda x.\\, \\lambda y.\\, x)\\ ((\\lambda z.\\, z)\\ w)$ by name. Is the argument ever evaluated?",
      "hints": [
        "The argument goes in unevaluated, under $\\lambda y$, where call by name does not look."
      ],
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "cbn: (λx. x x) ((λy. y) z)",
      "type": "exercise",
      "prompt": "Reduce $(\\lambda x.\\, x\\ x)\\ ((\\lambda y.\\, y)\\ z)$ by name. Where does it stop?",
      "hints": [
        "The argument goes in unevaluated, twice. Then the head is reduced, and only the head."
      ],
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "cbn: λx. (λy. y) x",
      "type": "exercise",
      "prompt": "Reduce $\\lambda x.\\, (\\lambda y.\\, y)\\ x$ by name.",
      "hints": [
        "Call by name does not reduce under a λ."
      ],
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "> [!summary]\n> A strategy answers \"which redex first?\". Normal order finds every normal form there is. Call by name and call by value stop at functions; call by value evaluates arguments first, once, and can loop on an argument nobody needs.\n\nEvery strategy here renames bound variables to substitute, and lesson 2 had to compare terms up to renaming. Names are a nuisance: can we do without them? Lesson 6.",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    }
  ],
  "scenes": []
}
